35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The late-turn pool is empty. Overtime covers everything. The lift contract has spare capacity, though.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, the audits prove it. Minutes vanish in waiting, not queues. The timed runs show surface delays, not platform crush.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The ledger is a graveyard. Ring-fenced money is frozen, and operating subsidies are shrinking. We are borrowing against a future that is already bankrupt.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The accessibility programme shows four lifts finished on time, but two lane-priority stretches were reverted. The pilots survived, but the infrastructure did not.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,The shells hide voids that stop work for weeks. The committee minutes show the ferry and rail back offices still disagree on data terms.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, if the ledger is frozen, we cannot fund the overtime that is currently propping up the empty rosters.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Benjamin, frozen money is a trap. My data shows the real cost is waiting time. We must fix the connections, not just the surface.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Takumi, exactly. The recurring fees are the real debt. We are paying for the privilege of being late, not for the fix.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Takumi, the pilots prove small, contained changes survive. The lane reverts show grand schemes fail. We must choose the durable, not the ambitious.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, contained changes are wise, but the committee minutes show the ferry and rail back offices still disagree on data terms. That is a hidden veto.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, if the back offices disagree, any plan needing their sign-off is dead. I need to know which candidates avoid that veto.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, that veto kills big projects. My data says the fix is simple: better connections. We need a plan that works without their permission.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, the veto is real, but the fees are the silent killer. We cannot fund the fix if the contractors keep eating the margin.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, the pilots survived because they were self-contained. We must avoid plans that depend on the frozen ledger or the divided back offices.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Asuka, the veto is real. Any plan touching ferry or rail data is stalled. We must pick something the authority controls alone.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,I am ready to see the candidates. We need a plan that fits the spare lift capacity and avoids the empty late-turn rosters.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"I am ready. The data is clear: fix the waiting, not the noise. I will vote for the plan that actually saves minutes.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,I am ready. I will vote for the plan that does not bleed us dry in year three.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
